Vertigo (BPPV/Meniere's/Vestibular Neuritis)
Opthalmology & ENT
Rotational spinning sensation, Provoked by head movement, Horizontal/rotatory nystagmus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-048
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe rotational dizziness with intractable vomiting and inability to walk or stand safely.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute onset of severe, incapacitating rotational vertigo resulting in severe nausea, recurrent vomiting, and a complete loss of postural stability. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to differentiate a benign peripheral vestibular crisis (Vestibular Neuritis) from an acute central posterior circulation stroke (cerebellar infarction), and to provide immediate parenteral antiemetic therapy to prevent severe dehydration before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The room is spinning so fast I can't even open my eyes, and every time I move my head I vomit."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports waking up 4 hours ago with a sudden, unremitting illusion of violent rotational movement of the surrounding environment. Endorses continuous, severe symptoms that are noticeably exacerbated by any rapid changes in head position or rolling over in bed. Accompanied by acute, severe nausea and frequent non-bilious vomiting. Reports a recent viral upper respiratory infection one week prior. Explicitly denies fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, or aural fullness. Denies facial droop, slurred speech, double vision, or weakness in the limbs.
Functional Status: Bedbound, lying perfectly still, actively vomiting into a basin.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; spouse is highly anxious regarding a suspected stroke.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Severely distressed, lying rigidly on one side, keeping eyes tightly closed.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals spontaneous, unidirectional horizontal nystagmus with a rotatory component; the fast phase beats strictly away from the left (affected) side. The nystagmus suppresses slightly with visual fixation. Cranial nerves are otherwise entirely intact; no dysarthria, normal facial symmetry. Cerebellar testing (finger-to-nose) is remarkably intact and accurate despite the severe subjective dizziness. Otoscopy reveals perfectly normal tympanic membranes bilaterally. Gross hearing is intact and symmetric to finger rub. Postural instability noted; patient leans heavily toward the left side when attempting to sit upright.
Environmental Observations: Bucket next to the bed; room is darkened.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Vestibular Neuritis - Status: Acute, Severe Crisis
Differentials:

Cerebellar Stroke / Posterior Circulation TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of central neurological signs (no dysarthria, diplopia, or severe ataxia in the limbs), the fact that the nystagmus is unidirectional and suppresses with visual fixation (central nystagmus often changes direction and does not suppress), and the absence of occipital headache.

Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The vertigo being continuous and unremitting even when perfectly still, rather than brief (<1 minute) episodic attacks strictly provoked by specific head movements.

Meniere's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute absence of fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, roaring tinnitus, or severe aural fullness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Dehydration and severe fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented completely incapacitated by an acute, violent vertiginous syndrome. The immediate and absolute clinical priority was to rule out a cerebellar or brainstem stroke. Careful bedside neurological examination definitively excluded central pathology, noting intact cerebellar limb function and a classic peripheral pattern of nystagmus. The history of a recent viral illness combined with continuous, prolonged vertigo without hearing loss firmly established the diagnosis of Vestibular Neuritis. By definitively ruling out a stroke, the urgent visit prevented a traumatic emergency department transfer, relieved extreme family anxiety, and provided immediate, necessary parenteral symptom control in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Prochlorperazine 12.5mg STAT to provide rapid, potent vestibular suppression and halt the intractable vomiting. Prescribed a short 3-day course of oral Prochlorperazine 5mg TDS for ongoing severe symptoms.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote rapid central nervous system compensation once the severe vomiting stops, the patient must purposefully focus their eyes on a fixed target (e.g., a picture frame) across the room while very slowly turning their head side to side. The active visual fixation suppresses the dizzy sensation (positive reinforcement), directly forcing the brain to recalibrate its balance pathways and dramatically speeding up the long-term recovery process compared to lying completely in the dark.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days. Vestibular physiotherapy referral if symptoms persist beyond 2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops slurred speech, double vision, sudden inability to walk (severe ataxia), facial drooping, or weakness in an arm or leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a viral nerve inflammation; full recovery of balance takes time.
Clinical Course: The severe spinning and vomiting will significantly decrease over the next 24-48 hours with the medication. However, you will likely feel "off-balance" or mildly dizzy when moving your head quickly for several weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly experience difficulty speaking, see double, or lose strength in your limbs, this is not a viral ear issue; it is a stroke, and you must call 000 immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A recent cold virus has severely inflamed the balance nerve in your left ear. It is sending a false signal to your brain that you are spinning violently. The injection sedates that nerve to stop the vomiting. Your brain will eventually learn to ignore the false signal.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely tonight. Tomorrow, you must begin slowly moving your head and keeping your eyes open to force your brain to adapt to the new balance signals. Take the anti-nausea tablets regularly for the next 3 days.

